[{:db/id 17592186105745 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-66e690c7-cd96-576d-8320-9b08eb6ce160"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105602 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n![](images/97/9773630254747a4bbd072bd44a6263bdd078fa09005b1bb5f51c48b894d67daf.svg)\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n![](images/43/43b9b089e0f93e63acf386bb605a0e3751cc8660899ce19d23835f9f79918bcc.svg)\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx - 3y = 1.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=1+3y,$ and the general solution is\n\n![](images/4a/4a598a80f2f14715d1f10d182b3f80887c7586beabecc2a329119b624c19a4fa.svg)\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=⟨1+3y,\\,y⟩,\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105746 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f4b75945-9982-5318-bfc3-2aef2116e166" "question-e2f37bf7-c408-5d68-a145-8a36082b4969" "question-2dc4858f-be36-5c29-9f4f-6c819c499765"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105603 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n![](images/bc/bcbd79c8cac28ef6bb4dc07a04789692d9726df238b4907841825244432c5783.svg)\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n![](images/d3/d381e04f0a7eb2973e20c9f397ef2e2267f90ac6992cfacd3a8927b4d48138a9.svg)\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{5}{2} & 2 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{5}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](images/89/89ca081f99d8cbd33bb11e58ef6aa91aa3045a02ff06c9877a53b9e2f88874db.svg)\n\nHowever, the final equation $0x+0y=-8$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=-8,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-66e690c7-cd96-576d-8320-9b08eb6ce160" :question/id #uuid "66e690c7-cd96-576d-8320-9b08eb6ce160" :question/math-academy-id "q-73741" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -5 & -10 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -5 & -10 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -10 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b7896c9b-7e0a-54c6-99f6-7e5e3f97a0cf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b7896c9b-7e0a-54c6-99f6-7e5e3f97a0cf" :answer-field/id #uuid "b7896c9b-7e0a-54c6-99f6-7e5e3f97a0cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1ee7bcb0-cbaf-5352-92b0-cd166136549a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-80f48683-4475-5276-86de-70b5aaa6717e" "answer-5995deb2-fa58-566a-aaf9-2112bbd190f6" "answer-1ee7bcb0-cbaf-5352-92b0-cd166136549a" "answer-4c9f17f4-9dc8-5361-a789-bdbc15bc853f" "answer-584317fd-30c4-5e13-a6b5-997c0e64fc09"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80f48683-4475-5276-86de-70b5aaa6717e" :answer/id #uuid "80f48683-4475-5276-86de-70b5aaa6717e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5995deb2-fa58-566a-aaf9-2112bbd190f6" :answer/id #uuid "5995deb2-fa58-566a-aaf9-2112bbd190f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ee7bcb0-cbaf-5352-92b0-cd166136549a" :answer/id #uuid "1ee7bcb0-cbaf-5352-92b0-cd166136549a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c9f17f4-9dc8-5361-a789-bdbc15bc853f" :answer/id #uuid "4c9f17f4-9dc8-5361-a789-bdbc15bc853f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-584317fd-30c4-5e13-a6b5-997c0e64fc09" :answer/id #uuid "584317fd-30c4-5e13-a6b5-997c0e64fc09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f4b75945-9982-5318-bfc3-2aef2116e166" :question/id #uuid "f4b75945-9982-5318-bfc3-2aef2116e166" :question/math-academy-id "q-42154" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+9y=12 \\\\ 4x+12y=13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 & 12 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 & 12 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=4 \\\\ 0x+0y=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0x+0y=-3$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=-3,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-10ecc780-6036-5f50-b353-d7aef2a64027"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-10ecc780-6036-5f50-b353-d7aef2a64027" :answer-field/id #uuid "10ecc780-6036-5f50-b353-d7aef2a64027" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-71d7b8c8-7f2d-57b3-ac13-87f27decfab8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e2d961c5-1118-567e-89ed-712103287b0f" "answer-6b96e348-3bdc-514b-ab89-33c93368576e" "answer-af263a57-923f-57e9-9a38-19e80e0ea6b1" "answer-42e1d594-9548-59e5-aad3-072205726cc7" "answer-71d7b8c8-7f2d-57b3-ac13-87f27decfab8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2d961c5-1118-567e-89ed-712103287b0f" :answer/id #uuid "e2d961c5-1118-567e-89ed-712103287b0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b96e348-3bdc-514b-ab89-33c93368576e" :answer/id #uuid "6b96e348-3bdc-514b-ab89-33c93368576e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3-4y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af263a57-923f-57e9-9a38-19e80e0ea6b1" :answer/id #uuid "af263a57-923f-57e9-9a38-19e80e0ea6b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42e1d594-9548-59e5-aad3-072205726cc7" :answer/id #uuid "42e1d594-9548-59e5-aad3-072205726cc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4-3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71d7b8c8-7f2d-57b3-ac13-87f27decfab8" :answer/id #uuid "71d7b8c8-7f2d-57b3-ac13-87f27decfab8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No solution" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e2f37bf7-c408-5d68-a145-8a36082b4969" :question/id #uuid "e2f37bf7-c408-5d68-a145-8a36082b4969" :question/math-academy-id "q-42163" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-3y=2 \\\\ -12x+36y=-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 36 & -28\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 36 & -28\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+12{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-3y=2 \\\\ 0x+0y=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0x+0y=-4$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=-4,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-43e2c6f5-42ea-508c-b13d-901ea5805cec"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-43e2c6f5-42ea-508c-b13d-901ea5805cec" :answer-field/id #uuid "43e2c6f5-42ea-508c-b13d-901ea5805cec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-58b4498d-2ddc-529c-be73-05b088efbf2a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-92f05ff5-b5f3-56d1-a8fc-bafcb1f20586" "answer-c9b78113-0b67-57ed-89a9-9f9bfbc9d657" "answer-501e28b0-b7a6-5c4a-b93c-c9142a4f1dd7" "answer-6087853b-8e40-57de-a8a4-4d62922fe815" "answer-58b4498d-2ddc-529c-be73-05b088efbf2a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92f05ff5-b5f3-56d1-a8fc-bafcb1f20586" :answer/id #uuid "92f05ff5-b5f3-56d1-a8fc-bafcb1f20586" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9b78113-0b67-57ed-89a9-9f9bfbc9d657" :answer/id #uuid "c9b78113-0b67-57ed-89a9-9f9bfbc9d657" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2-3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-501e28b0-b7a6-5c4a-b93c-c9142a4f1dd7" :answer/id #uuid "501e28b0-b7a6-5c4a-b93c-c9142a4f1dd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6087853b-8e40-57de-a8a4-4d62922fe815" :answer/id #uuid "6087853b-8e40-57de-a8a4-4d62922fe815" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2-3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58b4498d-2ddc-529c-be73-05b088efbf2a" :answer/id #uuid "58b4498d-2ddc-529c-be73-05b088efbf2a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No solution" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2dc4858f-be36-5c29-9f4f-6c819c499765" :question/id #uuid "2dc4858f-be36-5c29-9f4f-6c819c499765" :question/math-academy-id "q-20961" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+6y=10 \\\\ 3x+9y=21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 9 & 21\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 9 & 21\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 9 & 21\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=5 \\\\ 0x+0y=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0x+0y=6$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=6,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3a11f4ba-4a59-5d79-a194-7e373ec165ef"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3a11f4ba-4a59-5d79-a194-7e373ec165ef" :answer-field/id #uuid "3a11f4ba-4a59-5d79-a194-7e373ec165ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-24a55b62-4b9f-5002-bc93-434c7062356c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-77c9e10a-76a7-504f-a64c-28abc14cbdf3" "answer-1c7eb0bc-15c6-5831-a79d-5637e623be55" "answer-866d1061-3817-5b72-8cbe-b331346658ea" "answer-c85f2600-8ba5-58c2-bcb2-32e591add98e" "answer-24a55b62-4b9f-5002-bc93-434c7062356c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77c9e10a-76a7-504f-a64c-28abc14cbdf3" :answer/id #uuid "77c9e10a-76a7-504f-a64c-28abc14cbdf3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c7eb0bc-15c6-5831-a79d-5637e623be55" :answer/id #uuid "1c7eb0bc-15c6-5831-a79d-5637e623be55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5+3y \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-866d1061-3817-5b72-8cbe-b331346658ea" :answer/id #uuid "866d1061-3817-5b72-8cbe-b331346658ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c85f2600-8ba5-58c2-bcb2-32e591add98e" :answer/id #uuid "c85f2600-8ba5-58c2-bcb2-32e591add98e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5-3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24a55b62-4b9f-5002-bc93-434c7062356c" :answer/id #uuid "24a55b62-4b9f-5002-bc93-434c7062356c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No solution" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107080 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a system of linear equations where the corresponding coefficient matrix is singular. For example, consider the following system and its associated augmented matrix:\n![](images/6a/6a69daf7712312876b0ec97c540bbc472e46a7938beb8a59e694a04ad0b1d8c6.svg)\n\nTo solve the system, we can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as usual. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](images/a8/a8bfe3f6b27247ee9b27db8346fd94fb9068c85dec6f4a71517a821dee48dbf3.svg)\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx - 2y = -5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we describe the **general solution** of our system.\n\n- The first variable in our equation, $x,$ is called a **basic variable**.\n\n- The second variable in our equation, $y,$ is called a **free variable**.\n\nSolving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x = -5+2y.$ So the general solution of the system is\n![](images/0b/0ba4a232f29dd6fce2a8f6d7cda39b4f5b192c20fc91fe4403dfe648231382c5.svg)\nwhere the free variable $y$ is any number.\n\nIn vector form, this solution is written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5+2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is the equation of a straight line on a plane. Any point on this line satisfies our system of equations.\n\nIf we want to find some **particular solution** of the system, we simply choose some particular value of $y$ and find the corresponding value of $x$ on the line. For example, if we choose $y=0,$ then the corresponding value is $x=-5$ and the particular solution is $x=-5,$ $y=0.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107097 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, a system of equations might not have any solutions. For example, consider the following augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is\n![](images/e8/e8c64d8be247afd3e8ab02abc69e33635ef0234dbcbbc60f42379021a39a58a2.svg)\n\nThe final equation, $0x+0y=10,$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=10,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107081 :tutorial/content "Given a $2 \\times 2$ system of linear equations, we have the following possibilities for the number of solutions:\n\n- The system has exactly *one solution* if and only if the respective coefficient matrix is non-singular (meaning that the determinant is not zero). Such systems are called **consistent independent**.\n\n- The system has *infinitely many solutions* if the second row in the standard echelon form contains only $0$'s. These systems are called **consistent dependent**.\n\n- The system has *no solution* if the second row in the standard echelon form contains only $0$'s on the left-hand side and a non-zero element on the right-hand side. These are called **inconsistent** systems.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\n**Note:** A system of linear equations of the form \n![](images/cb/cb397dd01794f28ff11c01079e287d63175e439c8bbaa85cd02dc61689780fa8.svg)\nwhere we have only $0$'s on the right-hand side, is called **homogeneous**. A homogeneous system always has at least one solution, namely, the solution $x=0$ and $y=0.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle 0,0 \\rangle.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106000 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
